msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:174
msgid "We attach articulations and fingerings after a note: ``-.`` is staccato, ``->`` is an accent, and ``-1`` selects the first finger. Dynamics set the intensity, and a hairpin continues until ``\\!`` or the next dynamic. We place a small grace group before a main note with ``\\grace``; ``\\acciaccatura`` adds a slash, while ``\\afterGrace`` places the group after its main note. Grace time does not extend the measure. ``\\tempo \"Andante\" 4 = 88`` prints the text and, in parentheses, a note symbol for the beat with the rate, as LilyPond prints \"Andante (quarter note = 88)\"; ``\\tempo 4 = 88`` prints the metronome mark alone. ``\\breathe`` after a note prints a breath mark, a comma above the staff, and makes room for it before the next note."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:189
msgid "Andante, quarter note equals 88; grace notes before an F with a fingering and staccato, an accented G, A and B under a crescendo, a breath mark, then a loud whole-note C."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:189
msgid "A tempo with a metronome mark, grace notes, articulations, dynamics and a breath mark."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:197
msgid "We write a volta repeat once and give each ending in ``\\alternative``; ``\\volta 1,2 { ... }`` labels combined endings, and ``\\fine`` or ``\\section`` marks a structural double bar. The example has two passes with different final measures. ``\\repeat unfold`` expands its body instead of drawing repeat signs, and ``\\compressEmptyMeasures`` or ``\\compressFullBarRests`` is accepted for multi-measure rests. Chord names use a separate ``\\new ChordNames \\chordmode`` context beside the staff, with spellings such as ``c1:maj7``, ``d:m7`` and ``g:7``. They print as LilyPond's default chord names: ``g:7`` is G with a raised 7, ``c:maj7`` is C with a small raised triangle, and sharps and flats are music symbols."
msgstr ""

