#: ../../topics/music.rst:282
msgid "Write guitar tablature"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:285
msgid "A guitar part usually pairs a staff with tablature. ``\\new TabStaff`` draws six lines, one for each string of a guitar in standard tuning, with the highest string at the top, and a fret number on a string for each note. We write the same music for the staff and the tablature; guitar music sounds an octave below its written pitch, so the staff uses ``\\clef \"treble_8\"``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:298
msgid "A guitar staff above six-line tablature; the notes E, D, C, D are played on frets 2, 0, 3 and 0, then a C major chord and two notes on the second string."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:298
msgid "A guitar part in standard notation and tablature."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:307
msgid "Each note goes on the highest string that can play it, as LilyPond chooses by default: notes of a chord take the strings from the highest note down, each at fret 0 through 24. A string number after a note, such as ``c'4\\2``, names the string instead; inside a chord each note takes its own, as in ``<c\\5 e\\4>2``. On the staff above, each string number prints as a circled digit over its note, or under it in a voice with downward stems. A note that no free string can play, such as a C below the low E string, is refused with a placed diagnostic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:312
msgid "The tab staff shows strings, fret numbers and bar lines with a TAB clef, as LilyPond's default tablature does. It omits key and time signatures, stems, beams, rests, ties, slurs and scripts, which the staff above shows; a breath mark appears on both. A tied note is not repeated, except at the start of a line, where its fret number stands in parentheses. ``\\new TabVoice`` holds one voice of a tab staff, and voices on a tab staff share its strings. Other tunings and instruments, rhythm in the tab staff and fret diagrams are outside the profile. :doc:`../howto/songbook` writes a complete song for voice, piano and guitar."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:319
msgid "Read local files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:322
msgid "A directive can name a local file instead of containing an inline body. We use exactly one source form."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:331
msgid "Nested ``\\include`` paths resolve beside the including file, then through the source directory and configured ``include_roots``. Resolved paths must stay inside the allowed roots, including after symbolic links are checked. The build tracks source files, nested includes, missing local lookups and selected text font files by content. Changing a dependency rebuilds the generated image. Identical output bytes retain the same asset name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:337
msgid "``--untrusted`` permits inline notation because the reader runs no program or Scheme code. It refuses file arguments and filesystem includes before opening them. Trusted text output and fallback code blocks substitute external ``.ly`` source without engraving. When the file cannot be read, as in an untrusted build, the output names the score file in a sentence instead of showing its path as code. A failed drawing retains source code with a warning; ``-W`` stops publication and preserves the preceding output."
msgstr ""

