#: ../../topics/music.rst:362
msgid "``auto`` keeps a short final system at its natural width; ``all`` stretches every system; ``none`` avoids stretching."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:364
msgid "The project defaults are ``lilypond_staff_size``, ``lilypond_html_width`` and ``lilypond_pdf_width``. Their numeric values are typographic points. See :doc:`../reference/configuration` for the setting contract."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:368
msgid "When a drawing is wider than 225 points, HTML also receives a narrow edition engraved at 225 points with a staff height no greater than 14 points. The browser selects it below 40 rem through a ``picture`` element. This edition has its own system breaks. When its system limit prevents it from being drawn, the wide image scales to the screen. SVG adapts its ink to the page's color scheme; PNG keeps a light background panel in dark mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:374
msgid "PDF derives its measure from ``pdf_paper_size`` (``a4`` or ``letter``; any other value renders as A4) and the selected template's horizontal margin, then places music at its natural size. Built-in templates declare their horizontal margin in their leading 256 lines with a comment such as ``// guidedog: margin-x = 31mm``. A custom template without the declaration uses a 25 millimeter margin assumption. ``lilypond_pdf_width`` overrides the derived measure. Oversized music scales to fit with a build warning. A score with several systems can break between systems across PDF pages, with its caption after the last system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:381
msgid "Fonts, limits and references"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:384
msgid "Music uses the unmodified Bravura 1.482 font and SMuFL metadata under the SIL Open Font License. Text follows ``pdf_fonts`` and ``pdf_font_paths``, with regular, bold and italic fallback faces. Glyphs become outlines, so browsers and PDF readers need no installed music font. PNG export requires the embedded renderer; SVG engraving remains available in builds without it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:389
msgid "For CJK lyrics, we include a suitable CJK text face through ``pdf_font_paths``. When building this repository, ``cargo xtask fetch-fonts --music`` installs the pinned Noto Serif CJK face used by the mixed-script music corpus in ``docs/shared/fonts``. We can use that directory as a font path in our project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:393
msgid "One example accepts at most 256 KiB of source including includes, 32 included files, depth 64, 20,000 events, 24 staves, four voices sounding at once on a staff and 16 heads per chord. The reader and engraver each have a work limit of eight million operations. Expanded reader allocations have a separate 64 MiB allowance. A drawing has at most 64 systems and a height of 20,000 points. PNG is limited to 16 million pixels and 16,384 pixels on either side. A limit diagnostic identifies the source and suggests splitting the example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/music.rst:400
msgid "For notation syntax, we consult the `LilyPond 2.24 notation reference <https://lilypond.org/doc/v2.24/Documentation/notation/>`_. For glyph definitions, we consult the `Standard Music Font Layout specification <https://www.w3.org/2021/03/smufl14/>`_ and the `Bravura project <https://github.com/steinbergmedia/bravura>`_. Engraving references include Elaine Gould's *Behind Bars* and Ted Ross's *The Art of Music Engraving and Processing*. For other kinds of drawing, we use :doc:`diagrams`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:1
msgid "How a project works"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:4
msgid "The project root is the directory containing ``conf.toml``. ``root_doc`` names the document that starts the reading order. Guidedog discovers source documents, reads them, resolves their relations, and publishes a complete output generation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:11
msgid "Pipeline traverses discovery, source plugins, parallel reading, publication gate, reference resolution, rendering, HTML plugins, and atomic staging."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:11
msgid "Guidedog's end-to-end documentation and static site compilation pipeline."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:36
msgid "Documents and resources"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:39
msgid "A source suffix selects a reader. RST and project Markdown share the project graph. Images and downloads are resources. Guidedog copies resources that the documents use. Theme assets are listed in ``html_static_path``. An include must remain within the source directory or the configured ``include_roots``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:44
msgid "Separate projects and mounted sites"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:47
msgid "We keep a manual and design records in separate projects. They have different readers and different reading orders. Each project has its own configuration, templates, cache, and output. The Guidedog repository uses ``docs/manual`` and ``docs/gds`` this way, and mounts them into the root website in ``docs/conf.toml`` via ``[[site.mounts]]``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:49
msgid "Incremental work"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:52
msgid "Guidedog records the inputs and lookups used by each output. A changed source is reread. A changed dependency invalidates its users. A changed template invalidates the pages whose template chain loaded it. An unchanged document may be loaded from its saved object, including withheld drafts and scheduled posts that become eligible on a later build clock."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:54
msgid "The cache is evidence about a previous build. It is not the source of truth. ``-E`` discards the saved environment for the next build. ``-a`` writes all outputs. They solve different problems."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:58
msgid "\\text{reuse} = \\text{same inputs} \\land \\text{same lookups} \\land \\text{output exists}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:62
msgid "An explicit timestamp in a template is also an input to the rendered page. A page that prints the current minute can change even when its prose does not. We use ``SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH`` when we need a fixed build time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:66
msgid "Publication"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/projects.rst:69
msgid "A build writes changes to a sibling staging directory. It completes that directory, flushes the files and records, and then switches generations. Readers see a complete previous or new publication. An interrupted commit is settled on the next build. The design and its proof obligations are in :doc:`../internals/publication`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:1
msgid "Typography for reading"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:4
msgid "A page should make its structure visible before the reader studies it. A strong title, a quiet navigation column, and a measured text column do most of the work. Color should mark a relation or an action. It should not carry the meaning alone."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:8
msgid "The web page"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:11
msgid "The custom layout has three regions: project navigation, the document, and the current page's outline. Small screens use a navigation drawer. The reading edition bundles Libertinus Serif and Libertinus Sans. Titles use a bold serif face. Navigation, labels, and controls use its sans companion. The compressed WOFF2 files are served with the site. There are no font-service requests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:20
msgid "Navigation column on left, main document text column in center, local section outline on right."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:20
msgid "Three-region reading layout for HTML documentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:36
msgid "The built-in theme follows the operating system by default, with light and dark choices; Guidedog's own documentation website defaults to the light reading edition. The theme control remembers the reader's choice on the same browser origin. Printed HTML uses light colors. Content and navigation remain usable without JavaScript. The theme control, enhanced search, and paragraph line breaking are progressive enhancements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:43
msgid "Every initialized project (``guidedog quickstart``, ``guidedog ssg init``, and ``guidedog dsc init``) copies ``_static/typography.js`` beside ``_static/guidedog.css`` and ``_static/guidedog.js`` so a project owns and can edit its scripts directly. On desktop reading measures (``40rem`` and wider), English prose uses paragraph-wide **Knuth-Plass line breaking** in the browser across paragraphs, list items, definitions, admonitions, block quotes, footnotes, and post lists, including their cards, while leaving tables, navigation trees, metadata headers, and figure captions ragged. The script measures the loaded font, builds boxes, glue, and hyphenation penalties, and chooses breaks for the whole block. Frank Liang's pattern method supplies English hyphenation candidates, and break penalty widths are discarded after a hyphenated break so following lines are measured accurately. Inline mathematics (after MathJax finishes), footnote references, superscripts, and subscripts are measured as inline atoms; links keep their markup, and inline code literals may break cleanly after ``/`` or ``::``. The original TeX patterns and the libraries' BSD notices are recorded in ``NOTICE``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:57
msgid "The enhancement preserves emphasis and the exact source text. It recalculates when the reading measure changes. Copying within or across typeset blocks gives the source text, without visual line divisions or added hyphens. Narrow screens (below ``40rem``) and printing restore ordinary layout so mobile browsers wrap naturally and print engines can paginate paragraphs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:63
msgid "Other languages, narrow measures, JavaScript-disabled browsers, and paragraphs for which no suitably spaced solution exists use native browser layout with ``text-wrap: pretty`` (without greedy CSS justification or browser ``hyphens: auto``). That CSS property alone is not a promise of Knuth-Plass optimization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:68
msgid "The PDF page"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:71
msgid "The book and standalone article templates use the same Libertinus family as the web edition. They set justified paragraphs, optimized line breaking, and language-aware hyphenation. They also control spacing, running heads, captions, code blocks, and table rules. The font, hyphenation cost, and paragraph measure work together. A forced line break is rarely the right repair for a loose paragraph."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:73
msgid "Typst optimizes the PDF paragraphs. The web enhancement optimizes eligible prose. They share the source, fonts, and visual hierarchy. Their line breaks can differ. See the `Typst text contract <https://typst.app/docs/reference/text/text/>`_ and the `browser hyphenation contract <https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/hyphens>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:78
msgid "Change the design"
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:81
msgid "Edit ``_static/guidedog.css`` (or a project stylesheet listed in ``html_css_files``) for the documentation project's visual tokens. Edit ``_templates/layout.html`` for structure. Edit ``_templates/theme.typ`` for the PDF palette and typography. Edit ``_templates/book.typ`` for book structure, ``_templates/discussion.typ`` for discussion structure, and ``_templates/article.typ`` for standalone article PDFs. All three layouts wrap their content with the shared language-aware theme. Keep reading order, focus indicators, labels, and sufficient contrast intact."
msgstr ""

#: ../../topics/typography.rst:83
msgid "Build both targets after a change. Inspect a page with code, a table, a diagram, and an equation. An attractive cover is not proof that the rest of the book works. We build our own design in :doc:`../tutorial/a-custom-theme`. The full template contract is in :doc:`../templates`. The Rust API of Guidedog's crates is documented in the separate ``docs/api`` project."
msgstr ""

